## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design incremental data structures that handle batch updates with strong worst-case guarantees, and to reason about amortized costs versus rebuilding from scratch.

## Key Concepts
- DSU with parity (union-find with bipartite tracking)
- Incremental graph property maintenance
- Amortized analysis (inverse Ackermann function)
- Batch processing optimization

## Code Example
```javascript
class ParityDSU {
  constructor(n) {
    this.parent = Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) => i);
    this.parity = new Array(n).fill(0);
    this.rank = new Array(n).fill(0);
    this.isBipartite = true;
  }

  find(x) {
    if (this.parent[x] !== x) {
      const root = this.find(this.parent[x]);
      this.parity[x] ^= this.parity[this.parent[x]];
      this.parent[x] = root;
    }
    return this.parent[x];
  }

  union(x, y) {
    const rootX = this.find(x);
    const rootY = this.find(y);
    if (rootX === rootY) {
      if (this.parity[x] === this.parity[y]) {
        this.isBipartite = false;
      }
      return;
    }
    if (this.rank[rootX] < this.rank[rootY]) {
      this.parent[rootX] = rootY;
      this.parity[rootX] = this.parity[x] ^ this.parity[y] ^ 1;
    } else if (this.rank[rootX] > this.rank[rootY]) {
      this.parent[rootY] = rootX;
      this.parity[rootY] = this.parity[x] ^ this.parity[y] ^ 1;
    } else {
      this.parent[rootY] = rootX;
      this.rank[rootX]++;
      this.parity[rootY] = this.parity[x] ^ this.parity[y] ^ 1;
    }
  }

  processBatch(edges) {
    for (const [u, v] of edges) {
      this.union(u, v);
    }
    return this.isBipartite;
  }
}
```

## Comparison to Recomputing from Scratch
- **DSU with parity**: O(B α(n)) per batch, O(1) query
- **BFS/DFS recomputation**: O(n + m) per batch

The DSU approach provides a significant speedup when B << m, as it reuses information from previous batches rather than recomputing the entire graph structure.

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses your ability to design a memory-bounded streaming algorithm for heavy hitters with a fixed-coverage threshold.

## Key Concepts
- **Heavy Hitters:** Values that appear frequently in the stream
- **Streaming Algorithms:** Process data incrementally without storing the entire dataset
- **Bounded Memory:** Fixed-size data structures regardless of stream length
- **Threshold-Based Reporting:** Selecting the minimum subset that meets a coverage target

## Algorithm Analysis
- **Per-Element Update Cost:** O(log k) for heap maintenance (hash map lookup is O(1) average)
- **Per-Batch Query Cost:** O(k log k) to sort the heap and accumulate to the 95% threshold
- **Memory Usage:** O(M) for counts (if M is small) or O(unique values) with a hash map, plus O(k) for the heap

## Code Example
```javascript
class MinHeap {
  constructor(capacity) {
    this.heap = [];
    this.capacity = capacity;
  }
  push(item) {
    this.heap.push(item);
    this.bubbleUp(this.heap.length - 1);
    if (this.heap.length > this.capacity) this.pop();
  }
  pop() {
    const min = this.heap[0];
    const end = this.heap.pop();
    if (this.heap.length > 0) {
      this.heap[0] = end;
      this.bubbleDown(0);
    }
    return min;
  }
  bubbleUp(i) {
    while (i > 0) {
      const p = Math.floor((i - 1) / 2);
      if (this.heap[p].count <= this.heap[i].count) break;
      [this.heap[p], this.heap[i]] = [this.heap[i], this.heap[p]];
      i = p;
    }
  }
  bubbleDown(i) {
    while (true) {
      const l = 2 * i + 1, r = 2 * i + 2;
      let smallest = i;
      if (l < this.heap.length && this.heap[l].count < this.heap[smallest].count) smallest = l;
      if (r < this.heap.length && this.heap[r].count < this.heap[smallest].count) smallest = r;
      if (smallest === i) break;
      [this.heap[i], this.heap[smallest]] = [this.heap[smallest], this.heap[i]];
      i = smallest;
    }
  }
}

const counts = new Map();
let total = 0;
const k = 20;
const heap = new MinHeap(k);

function updateBatch(batch) {
  for (const v of batch) {
    const count = (counts.get(v) || 0) + 1;
    counts.set(v, count);
    total++;
    heap.push({ value: v, count });
  }
}

function get95Cover() {
  const sorted = [...heap.heap].sort((a, b) => b.count - a.count);
  const threshold = total * 0.95;
  let sum = 0;
  const result = [];
  for (const item of sorted) {
    sum += item.count;
    result.push(item.value);
    if (sum >= threshold) break;
  }
  if (sum < threshold) {
    return [...counts.keys()];
  }
  return result;
}
```

## Trade-offs
The heap-only approach tracks only the top-k values, so if fewer than k values dominate the stream, we may not reach 95% coverage. In such cases, the algorithm falls back to returning all seen values. For guaranteed bounded memory with approximate coverage, consider probabilistic methods like Count-Min Sketch or Lossy Counting.

## Why This Is Asked
This tests basic algorithmic analysis and trade-offs between brute force and divide-and-conquer techniques.

## Key Concepts
- Time complexity and space usage
- Inversions problem and two standard approaches
- Trade-offs with input size and data order

## Code Example
```javascript
function countInversionsNaive(a){
  let c=0;
  for(let i=0;i<a.length;i++){
    for(let j=i+1;j<a.length;j++){
      if(a[i]>a[j]) c++;
    }
  }
  return c;
}
function countInversionsMergeSort(a){
  function sortCount(arr){
    if(arr.length<=1) return {array: arr, inv:0};
    const mid = Math.floor(arr.length/2);
    const left = sortCount(arr.slice(0,mid));
    const right = sortCount(arr.slice(mid));
    let i=0,j=0, merged=[], inv= left.inv + right.inv;
    while(i<left.array.length && j<right.array.length){
      if(left.array[i] <= right.array[j]) merged.push(left.array[i++]);
      else { merged.push(right.array[j++]); inv += left.array.length - i; }
    }
    return {array: merged.concat(left.array.slice(i)).concat(right.array.slice(j)), inv: inv};
  }
  return sortCount(a).inv;
}
```

